## reading
The control run (Q40) repeats Q38 without the radio having been touched: 0.69 MB/s,
500-561 fps, RSSI 77, zero drops. So the radio had not degraded when merge ON collapsed
to 33 fps. The difference is the merge itself: with join_us=400 the phone sends joined
frames and their CRC error rate is 40.3% versus 11-12% for single frames at the same MCS
(median 19). Two of the three "merge on" seconds also lose frames at the receiver, but
netd still reported input_fail=0 - the loss was on the air, not in software.

Conclusion for the configuration: at fixed 149/20 keep TCP merge OFF (-j 0). The
historical 1.12 MB/s used merge ON together with the social slot sequence, so merge is
not universally bad - it is bad at this fixed-channel condition, where bigger frames
cost 3.4x the CRC rate. That social+merge configuration with the Q38 netd is the next
single-variable run (it is also the supervisor default).

Note: Q38 and Q40 differ by 23% (0.85 vs 0.69 MB/s) with identical settings; that is
run-to-run variance of the phone/link, not a code difference.

